+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the density of non-empty blocks. With effective empty space skipping, complexity approaches $O(N_{occupied})$ rather than $O(N_{total})$ [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{total})$ for the volume data. Additional memory for acceleration structures (e.g., min/max values per block) is $O(N_{blocks})$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources for the general method. For sub-volume partitioning on Teramac, memory overhead due to boundary-voxel overlap is 61% for $p=8$, 34% for $p=16$, 18% for $p=32$, and 10% for $p=64$ in a $128^3$ dataset [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables interactive rendering of large datasets by skipping empty space and terminating rays early [S1]. Simple to implement for uniform blocks [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Sub-volume partitioning requires global connectivity between pipelines and has high memory overhead due to overlap for interpolation [S3]. Uniform blocks may not adapt well to varying data density [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When the volume has significant empty space or when interactive frame rates are required [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the volume is dense with no empty space, acceleration benefits are reduced [S1]. When hardware lacks global connectivity for sub-volume partitioning [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was employed by Xie et al. to subdivide volume datasets into uniform-sized blocks and apply acceleration techniques such as early ray termination, empty space skipping, and occlusion clipping to interactively display CT datasets [S1]. Related work includes using octrees to encode boxes for efficient empty space skipping [S1], exploiting slab silhouette maps and early depth tests to skip empty spaces [S1], and using kd-trees and dynamic programming to optimize non-empty space tracking and data distribution balance on volume-rendering clusters [S1].
+The technique was employed by Xie et al. to subdivide volume datasets into uniform-sized blocks and apply acceleration techniques like early ray termination, empty space skipping, and occlusion clipping for interactive display of CT datasets [S1]. Related work includes Keleş et al. using slab silhouette maps and early depth tests [S1], and Frank and Kaufman using kd-trees and dynamic programming for non-empty space tracking on clusters [S1]. The block-based acceleration structure for isosurface extraction was developed to enable interactive modeling and visualization of complex terrains [S2, unofficial]. Sub-volume partitioning was developed for the Teramac Custom Computing Machine to render large datasets using multiple processing elements [S3].
 
